 ## Overview
-Transvoxel is a level-of-detail (LOD) technique for isosurface extraction that maintains a watertight mesh when neighboring voxels in a hierarchical grid have different resolutions [S1, unofficial]. It solves the gap problem inherent in standard LOD transitions by introducing transition cells that interpolate vertices between coarse and fine grids, ensuring continuity across resolution boundaries [S1, unofficial]. Unlike standard Marching Cubes which assumes uniform voxel size, Transvoxel adapts vertex positions and normals based on the LOD of adjacent voxels [S1, unofficial].
+Transvoxel is an extension to the Marching Cubes technique that introduces a specific set of polygon configurations for "transition cells" to connect meshes extracted from voxel grids with different levels of detail (LOD) [S1]. It solves the problem of topological cracks and holes that occur when neighboring voxels have different resolutions, ensuring a watertight surface by adjusting vertex positions and normals at the boundaries of LOD changes [S2, unofficial]. Unlike standard Marching Cubes, which assumes a uniform grid, Transvoxel handles the geometric discontinuity between high-resolution and low-resolution regions by subdividing the transition zone into specific cell types [S1].
 
 ## How it works
-The method is implemented as a geometry shader that processes voxel data to generate a continuous surface [S1, unofficial].
